WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:02.902
 All right, Tony,
congratulations.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:03.903
You've made it into
the third level of

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.471
this multi-level competition.

00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:08.308
Now, the judge you're
competing against--

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:13.113
[tense music]

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:20.820
Oh, man, ABS master
smith J. Neilson,

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.422
The Beast from the East.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.557
You got your hands
full with this one.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:25.391
 Tony.

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:26.726
 Have fun.

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:27.993
WIL WILLIS: J., welcome back.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.562
You know how this goes.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:33.365
You guys have eight hours
to do a custom blade build.

00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:34.967
And at the end of that,
we're going to award

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.937
one of you a $10,000 prize.

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:40.173
Now, in this third
level of competition,

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:43.342
we're going to be asking you
guys to make short swords.

00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:45.944
They must measure between
20 and 22 inches in length.

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:47.280
They must have a
functional guard,

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:50.683
a mechanically-attached
handle, and a pommel.

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:54.187
That sounds easy enough, right?

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:56.389
But you'll be making
them out of these.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.827
[cloth flaps]

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.063
Saw blades.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:05.865
From these saw
blades, you must make

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:09.035
a canister Damascus
billet, but not

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:10.836
any canister Damascus built--

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.272
a no-can canister
Damascus billet.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:17.376
Which means that once you're
done forge-welding your can,

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:20.813
you must peel off the
outer layer of mild steel,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.783
and use only the high-carbon
steel from inside your cans

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.586
to forge your blades.

00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:27.587
All right, gents.

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:29.555
Your eight hours starts now.

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.290
TONY FETTERS: So I'm going
to take the saw blades.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.825
I'm going to stack
them vertically,

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:35.762
but I want to leave about a
3/16" gap between each blade

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:38.364
so that I can fill
it up with 1095.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.334
And that's going
to be a cushion.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.469
It's all that band saw steel.

00:01:43.535 --> 00:01:47.673
So I figured, hey, great, band
saw blades make hard steel.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.343
I've got these two billets
in that I've tacked together

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:52.778
in the forge, and I'm waiting.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:54.046
I get the right color.

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:55.181
[hammer tapping]

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:57.116
And I set the weld.

00:01:57.182 --> 00:01:59.318
And then I start
drawing out my billet.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.021
Did I know that I was going
to have almost twice as much

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:02.889
as I needed?

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:04.490
Yes.

00:02:04.556 --> 00:02:08.594
But I'm thinking in my head,
what if I have a little delam?

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:12.098
I can just cut that off and
draw back to the length I need

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:14.033
and I'm golden.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:18.138
[hard rock music]

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:26.813
[boom]

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:29.749
[thunk]

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.585
WIL WILLIS: Tony's
in the quench.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:36.556
 And I pull it out,
and yep, it's hard.

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:37.690
[chuckles]

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:38.892
WIL WILLIS: I think J.'s
going to go for quench.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.928
DOUG MARCAIDA: J just quenched.

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:44.263
MAN: All right, J quenched.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.564
J. NEILSON: I pulled the
blade out of the quench.

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:46.399
It's straight.

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:47.867
I'm happy.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:48.701
I'll check it.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:49.536
[scraping]

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:50.936
 That sounds good.

00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:52.337
 And it sings like a bird.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:55.274
WIL WILLIS: Bladesmiths,
you have just three hours

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.443
remaining to finish your work.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.844
J. NEILSON: You've
got to keep moving.

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.179
You can't stop.

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:01.681
So while the blade is
in the tempering oven,

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:03.849
I'm going to get back working
on the handle and hardware.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.351
[hammer tapping]

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.153 line:0%
I picked a mild steel
for the guard and pommel,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.121 line:0%
because I plan on blowing
both of them them.

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:10.289 line:0%
That's going to save
me a little time

00:03:10.355 --> 00:03:12.192 line:7%
in doing a high finish on it.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.961
TONY FETTERS: I'm
getting all the hardware

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:17.330
and handle skills fitted up.

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:18.664
And then I'm going
to get everything

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:21.100
profile down so everything
fits right and tight.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:22.335
J. NEILSON: I got,
like, 10, 12 minutes

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.504
left, and I double-check.

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:26.172
[bleep] over.

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:28.640
And I'm like 1/8 of an
inch over parameters.

00:03:28.707 --> 00:03:29.942
 Uh oh.

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:32.478
J. NEILSON: If I turn in a blade
that was out of parameters,

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.114
you might as well just
kick me off this show.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.116
[bleep] beautiful.

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:41.520
WIL WILLIS: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:44.223
Bladesmiths, shut down your
machines, drop your tools.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.626
This level of the
competition is over.

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:47.293
[clapping]

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:48.694
 Good job on that, man.

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:51.864
 All right, bladesmiths,
welcome to the KEAL test.

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:54.400
Now it's time to find out
what kind of lethal damage

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.235
your short swords will do.

00:03:56.301 --> 00:03:57.669
To do that, I will
take your weapons

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.306
and deliver some lethal
blows on this pig carcass.

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:01.207
Tony, you're up first.

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:01.907
You ready for this?

00:04:01.974 --> 00:04:04.043
 Absolutely.

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:07.913
[slicing]

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:11.918
[tense music]

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:18.258
[slice, flesh ripping]

00:04:18.957 --> 00:04:20.793
[heavy breathing]

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:23.662
 Nice cuts, man.

00:04:23.762 --> 00:04:24.497
 Thanks.

00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:26.032
I can tell it's heavy, though.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:27.333
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, Tony, let's talk

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.068
about your sword here.

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:30.069
It's a heavy beast.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.436
 Yes.

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:32.771
DOUG MARCAIDA: It's about
four pounds on this.

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:36.041
Requires more work to
wield multiple times.

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:41.547
But overall, it's a good chopper
and slasher, and it will KEAL.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.849
 [chuckles]

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:44.684
 All right, Jay.

00:04:44.751 --> 00:04:45.217
You're up next.

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:45.985
Are you ready?

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:46.718
 Do me a favor.

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:47.920
KEAL it.

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:48.987
 Will do.

00:04:49.054 --> 00:04:50.689
 I love the pig test.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.224 line:7%
[music playing]

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.759 line:7%
Tony's blade did great.

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:57.596 line:0%
But after 20-plus
years of making knives,

00:04:57.696 --> 00:04:59.399 line:0%
I'd better be confident
going into this.

00:05:03.869 --> 00:05:07.707
[slicing]

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:23.456
[flesh ripping]

00:05:23.522 --> 00:05:25.224
[heavy breathing]

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:25.958
 Good job.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:27.359
 Nice cuts.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.962
 All right, J., let's talk
about your short sword.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:33.666
First up, what I like about
this, it's a lighter sword.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.834
You can wield this
single-handedly

00:05:35.934 --> 00:05:39.138
and be able to do this all
day because of its weight.

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:42.174
Your handle
construction is smooth.

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:44.076
Everything's rounded.

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:46.745
And this is a sharp edge.

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:48.881
It cuts deeply into
this pig carcass--

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:51.650
not very deep because the
weight is not on the sword.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.187
I can wield it cut accordingly.

00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:58.156
More importantly, sir,
your sword, it'll KEAL.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:01.560
[tense music]

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:02.928
DAVID BAKER: All
right, gentlemen,

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:05.698
it's time for the strength
test, the pot shot.

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:08.400
Now, this test is all
about what those pots can

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.569
do to the edge of your blades.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:11.437
Tony, you're up first.

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:12.170
You ready for this?

00:06:12.237 --> 00:06:13.739
 Yes, I am.

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:18.911
[smashing]

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.952
[pot clatters]

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:27.686
 Didn't break.

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:29.421
 Nice.

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:32.157
 All right, so Tony,
everything's solid.

00:06:32.224 --> 00:06:33.592
Still together perfectly.

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.995
You've lost a little bit
of edge right through here.

00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:40.132
Just sort of packed out, as
opposed to chipped or rolled.

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:42.434
But it's a neat-looking design.

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:43.935
Think you did a great job.

00:06:44.002 --> 00:06:45.538
 Thank you.

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:47.172
 All right, J., your turn.

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:47.939
You ready?

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:48.708
 Go for it, man.

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:49.675
 [chuckles] OK.

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:54.947
[smashing]

00:07:02.789 --> 00:07:04.157
[pot clatters]

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:05.524
 Good job, buddy.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:06.792
 Well, it didn't break.

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:07.627
[chuckles]

00:07:09.027 --> 00:07:11.430
 All right, so J., I like
the balance of your blade.

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:14.733
It's forward, but it's
a light blade, so it's

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.402
comfortable being up there.

00:07:16.468 --> 00:07:19.204
You lost a little bit of edge.

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:21.207
Again, it's just
kind of compacted.

00:07:21.273 --> 00:07:22.742
The blade's solid.

00:07:22.809 --> 00:07:23.408
It did great.

00:07:23.475 --> 00:07:24.376
Feels good in the hand.

00:07:24.443 --> 00:07:26.278
Nicely done.

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:28.448
[music playing]

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:31.684
 Bladesmiths, to check
out how much of your edge

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.319
is still left on your
blades, I'm going to be

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:35.554
chopping at these sandmen.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:36.455
Tony, you're up first.

00:07:36.555 --> 00:07:37.389
You ready for this?

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:38.691
 Don't hurt yourself.

00:07:38.758 --> 00:07:40.092
[chuckles]

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:42.595
TONY FETTERS: At this
point the competition,

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:44.062
we're neck and neck.

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:48.233
And it's going to come
down to the sharpness test.

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:53.139
[thwacking]

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.845
 [sighs]

00:07:59.945 --> 00:08:04.183
 Well, Tony, there is a sort
of sweet spot to cut things.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:07.052
And this is where all of the
damage from the pots went.

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:09.388
And the first cut was
probably a little further

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:10.789
down into this sharp region.

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:12.824
And it cut cleanly
through the first arm.

00:08:12.924 --> 00:08:14.327
But when I came around
to start hitting

00:08:14.393 --> 00:08:16.661
on those hips and the
thighs, it was just

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:19.031
kind of bludgeoning the
sandman instead of really

00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:20.532
cutting in nice and deeply.

00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:23.769
Other than that, it's a
really interesting design.

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:26.572
But man, this is a
heavy, heavy sword.

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:29.742
All in all, it will
cut some stuff.

00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:30.743
 OK.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:31.543
[chuckles]

00:08:31.643 --> 00:08:32.745
 All right, J., you're up next.

00:08:32.812 --> 00:08:33.712
You ready?

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:35.781
All right, nonverbal
communication.

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:37.149
 [chuckles]

00:08:38.251 --> 00:08:42.722
[tense music]

00:08:45.524 --> 00:08:51.030
[thwacking]

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.839
 Well, that sucked.

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:02.007
[chuckles]

00:09:02.074 --> 00:09:04.409
 All right, J. I love
the weight of this weapon.

00:09:04.509 --> 00:09:06.679
I think it's perfectly balanced.

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:09.815
And that weight is
just a joy to swing.

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:11.651
It didn't cut very well
on a lot of the strikes.

00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:15.120
It did about halfway through on
the wrist, and then mostly just

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:16.722
scratches on the body.

00:09:16.789 --> 00:09:21.193
All in all, I think you made
a light, fast, fun weapon.

00:09:21.293 --> 00:09:24.162
So nice job.

00:09:24.229 --> 00:09:26.566
 All right, gents, the judges'
deliberation is complete.

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:30.769
They've made their
final decision.

00:09:30.869 --> 00:09:33.972
The winner of this Beat
the Judges competition is--

00:09:34.073 --> 00:09:38.244
[tense music]

00:09:40.713 --> 00:09:42.514
--J. Neilson.

00:09:42.614 --> 00:09:45.217
Congratulations.
$10,000 will be donated

00:09:45.317 --> 00:09:47.519
to the charity of your choice.

00:09:47.619 --> 00:09:48.587
What charity is that?

00:09:48.687 --> 00:09:49.855
 It's the Melanoma Foundation.

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:50.989
 All right.

00:09:51.056 --> 00:09:52.825
That is an excellent,
excellent foundation.

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:56.462
Tony, unfortunately your blade
didn't make the cut this time.

00:09:56.529 --> 00:09:58.397
 Tony, this came down
to a matter of weight.

00:09:58.497 --> 00:10:01.500
That short sword
weighs more than really

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:02.868
a two-hander should.
So--

00:10:02.968 --> 00:10:04.069
 Right.

00:10:04.169 --> 00:10:06.872
 --it did the job, but
that weight is just extreme.

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:08.139
 OK.

00:10:08.206 --> 00:10:08.674
 Tony, hey.

00:10:08.741 --> 00:10:09.407
 Hey, good job.

00:10:09.508 --> 00:10:10.142
 Good job, man.

00:10:10.242 --> 00:10:11.210
 Yeah.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:12.611
 Are you gonna hug?
Was that--

00:10:12.678 --> 00:10:13.979
- I was going to hug.
- I was gonna hug him.

00:10:14.046 --> 00:10:14.847
WIL WILLIS: Like, that
was an awkward moment.

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:15.447
We're too--

00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:16.381
[both chuckle]

00:10:16.448 --> 00:10:17.682
All right, good job, man.

00:10:17.749 --> 00:10:19.018
J. NEILSON: Tony is
a great competitor.

00:10:19.118 --> 00:10:20.252
He did a great piece.

00:10:20.319 --> 00:10:21.720
But I squeaked ahead of him.

00:10:21.786 --> 00:10:24.289
Got $10,000 going to
the Melanoma Foundation.

00:10:24.356 --> 00:10:25.591
I feel good.

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:27.126
TONY FETTERS: This time,
I didn't beat the judge.

00:10:27.226 --> 00:10:29.561
You know, it never feels good
to not win when you really go

00:10:29.628 --> 00:10:30.462
and bust ass.

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:32.664
But I've learned a lot--

00:10:32.765 --> 00:10:34.433
don't make your
blades too heavy.

00:10:34.533 --> 00:10:36.067
[chuckles]

00:10:36.134 --> 00:10:38.704
Good job, J., but watch
out for next time.

00:10:38.771 --> 00:10:39.705
I'm coming back.

00:10:39.772 --> 00:10:42.642
[music playing]

